  "text": "Question thirteen. What is gradient boosting? It adds shallow trees one at a time, each fitted to the negative gradient of the loss: for squared error, the residuals. Ex-gee-boost, LightGBM and CatBoost add sampling, L one and L two penalties, and histogram splits for speed. The follow-up is how many trees. On a synthetic benchmark, with each tree scaled by a learning rate of one, validation error is lowest after twelve trees, then climbs. At point one, it keeps falling until seven hundred and eighty-four, to less than half the error. Early stopping on validation picks the count, and every tree runs again for each prediction, so the count is also a latency budget.",
